# M2 VM lab infrastructure

Parent: [[Designs/Lab Capsule and Factory Design Plan]]. Review: [[Designs/Docker Architecture Review and Feasibility]]. M2 target architecture; M1 is the migration baseline.

## Concrete deployment

One lab is one Linux VM with a local Docker Engine. The factory is a module/worker of that lab's AI4R with independent jobs and versions. An operator-installed host broker owns Docker access. Compose runs the persistent app and model bridge; the broker creates short-lived sibling CC containers on that same engine. No Docker daemon runs inside a CC.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    P[Operator VM provisioner] --> V
    R[Tested M2 release and image registry] --> V
    subgraph V[Lab Linux VM]
        direction TB
        APP[Compose - AI4R app and local factory]
        MODEL[Compose - scoped model bridge]
        BROKER[Host broker - system service]
        ENGINE[Local Docker Engine]
        BUILDER[BuildKit builder container]
        CC[Short-lived CC containers]
        DISK[Project, jobs and sealed evidence]
        APP -->|authorized job API over Unix socket| BROKER
        BROKER --> ENGINE
        ENGINE --> BUILDER
        ENGINE --> CC
        CC -->|invocation-scoped IPC| MODEL
        BROKER --> DISK
        CC -->|staged input and output paths only| DISK
    end
    APP -.->|candidate source, image and evidence| Q[Central qualification]
```

Only broker/operator identities access the Docker socket. The app submits typed jobs with artifact IDs, approved image identity, limits and grants. Broker policy derives paths/options; callers cannot supply arbitrary Docker flags, host paths, devices or commands. Factory build credentials, protected dispatch credentials and local admission have distinct scopes.

The broker is privileged infrastructure. Docker control can affect the VM; generated builds execute code too. Each disposable lab VM is the starting outer boundary. A BuildKit container on that daemon is not a second VM boundary. A later adversarial-build profile may need a separate build VM. [Docker daemon security](https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/).

## Creating a new VM

Use a checksummed Linux cloud image, cloud-init and a versioned bootstrap/configuration script. Start with one Linux KVM/libvirt reference backend; cloud/Hyper-V are later provider adapters. Creating lab VMs inside Docker Desktop is not the deployment model.

1. Select lab/project IDs, architecture, VM size/storage/network and tested M2 release. Generate unique cloud-init instance ID, hostname and operator access. Never clone live sessions or credentials.
2. Create a fresh guest disk from the verified image; optionally attach a project-data disk. Boot with metadata/user-data, wait for cloud-init completion and record the provider identity. NoCloud uses instance identity to distinguish first boot. [NoCloud](https://docs.cloud-init.io/en/latest/reference/datasources/nocloud.html).
3. Install release-selected Docker Engine, Compose/Buildx and broker versions; create service accounts/directories, log rotation and quotas. Keep package versions in a tested release lock. [Engine installation](https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/ubuntu/).
4. Fetch the release manifest/Compose definition and source commit. Create a standalone Git clone and local development branch. Import seed CC/admission records and pull compatible images by registry digest. Seed resources separately.
5. Enroll scoped credentials outside Git/images/templates. Start the host broker and stable Compose services. Missing required pins or credentials produces a visible failed/blocked preparation state.
6. Check app/API health, broker authorization, platform/image availability, output ownership, effective limits, model route and a known CC invocation. Record evidence before READY; boot completion alone is insufficient.

Illustrative operator command on a prepared Linux virtualization host; this is not an implemented lab CLI and was not executed here. Paths/image/network must be prepared. The example assumes x86_64; ARM requires matching provider/image support.

```bash
virt-install --name ai4r-lab-a --memory 4096 --vcpus 2 \
  --os-variant detect=on,name=ubuntunoble \
  --disk size=40,backing_store=/srv/vm-images/verified-ubuntu-amd64.qcow2 \
  --cloud-init user-data=/srv/lab-specs/a/user-data,meta-data=/srv/lab-specs/a/meta-data \
  --network network=default --noautoconsole
```

Based on cloud-init's [libvirt procedure](https://docs.cloud-init.io/en/latest/howto/launch_libvirt.html). Verify provider behavior and retention during implementation. Resource numbers are a lightweight CPU test hypothesis, not validated AI4R requirements.

## Host paths and versioned state

```text
/opt/ai4r/releases/<release>/   compose and release metadata
/srv/ai4r/source/              standalone clone and local branch
/srv/ai4r/state/               database, catalog and factory ledger
/srv/ai4r/resources/           versioned datasets and prepared sources
/srv/ai4r/jobs/<attempt>/      staged inputs, outputs, temporary work
/srv/ai4r/evidence/            sealed records and verified exports
/run/ai4r-broker/              authenticated local sockets
Docker-managed storage        images/layers and BuildKit cache
```

These are VM-host paths. Broker maps artifact references to them; capsules see only invocation mounts. Separate ownership prevents capsule UID writes to sealed evidence, catalog or broker policy. Pre-create writable output directories for the capsule UID, rather than making broad directories world-writable.

Release execution uses an image built from a captured source tree. A development override may mount live source for fast iteration, but results are development-only until replayed against an immutable image. Cross-VM labs are standalone clones; Git worktree metadata from another machine is not copied. Record source revision and any uncommitted/untracked delta separately.

## Compose, jobs and access

Compose describes stable app/model-bridge services, versioned state mounts, health checks and restart policy. Factory initially runs inside the app; split a worker only when concurrency warrants it. The system broker owns dynamic CC containers and labels lab/run/attempt resources. Do not add a permanent Compose service for every generated CC. [Single-server Compose](https://docs.docker.com/compose/how-tos/production/).

Tool CCs start with no network. Agent CCs get invocation-scoped model IPC and no general app/database access. Use guest-loopback UI plus an operator tunnel initially. A private-LAN ingress is an explicit later deployment choice. A Compose network is not a destination allowlist; service DNS only works on attached networks. [Compose networking](https://docs.docker.com/compose/how-tos/networking/).

## Capacity, backup and recovery

Reserve headroom for app/OS/engine. Cap builds separately from CC concurrency. Memory/CPU/PID launch limits do not provide disk quotas or log retention; implement those separately. A shared model server is an external dependency, not shared lab catalog/source state.

Quiesce affected jobs and use a database-consistent backup procedure. Capture source/catalog/resource/evidence manifests and back up outside the VM. Saved images exclude mounted data; caches are not backups. Re-pull pinned images or archive selected local candidates with hashes. Restore into a new lab identity; retain historical run IDs as history. [Docker volumes](https://docs.docker.com/engine/storage/volumes/).

Broker persists launch intent and container identity, then reconciles its ledger with labeled Docker resources after restart. Retain stopped containers for diagnostic collection; never replay effects automatically. Cleanup requires verified export. Image/cache pruning must protect active/retained run pins and serialize with builds. Rollback restores a compatible app/catalog/database set, not just an older app image.

First VM acceptance: reproducible boot/readiness, two independent projects/factories, interrupted build/run recovery, evidence export, fresh-VM restore and selective update/rollback. GPU passthrough is a separate target-host trial; a guest container cannot expose hardware the guest lacks.
